Node ID=900 friend cortes
Text=Cortes came out of the desert with a mechanical arm, a share of a treasure, and the first clear accounts of his life. He paid Shylocke in full in front of witnesses and asked for the note back so he could burn it himself. The arm wanted a smith twice a year and he paid that too, out of the share, and never once asked DaVinci for a discount. He talked about the cave for the rest of his life, and about the river before it, and about the arm, and never mentioned the debt to anybody who had not already heard about it from Shylocke.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 friend cortes dead
Text=Cortes died with DaVinci's arm still on him, and it was the arm that came home. A Templar sergeant carried it back to Barcelona because it was obviously worth something, and DaVinci took it apart on the bench where he had built it and did not rebuild it. He kept the hand in a drawer and showed it to visitors for years, and told the story of it wrong every time. The debt was never paid and never pursued, Shylocke being a man who kept accounts and also kept score. The treasure stayed where it was. Nobody else in Barcelona knew where the cave was, because Cortes had told only you.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 friend cortes vindicated
Text=You heard both men out and found for the conquistador, and Shylocke did not get the ship. Cortes never sailed again regardless, having no crew and one arm and no credit anywhere in Barcelona, but he kept the ship tied up where he could see it from the tavern and paid harbour fees on it for nine years. He told the story of the hearing to everybody, and in his telling you are a judge rather than a stranger who happened to be standing there. Shylocke's version is shorter and he tells it less. Cortes is in the harbour ledgers for nine years running and in no other record of the city at all.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 friend cortes shylocke
Text=You found for the moneylender, and he took the ship inside a fortnight and sold it inside a month. Cortes said one thing about it at the time -- *"here I thought you would vindicate me"* -- and nothing afterwards, to you or about you. He drank in La Serpienta Vieja on credit extended out of pity by a landlord who had heard the story, and he never went back into the desert, and the treasure is still in the cave. Shylocke, who had been entirely within his rights, did not thank you either. The landlord's charity outlasted the moneylender's profit on the ship by about six years.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 friend cortes no verdict
Text=You declined to settle it and the two of them went on arguing, which suited Shylocke, time being the only thing in the dispute that was working for him. The case was heard eventually by a Templar magistrate who had no patience for either man and split the difference badly. Cortes kept half a ship and a debt he could not service. *"I suppose I should not have rested my hopes on a stranger,"* he had said, and he turned out to be right about that in a way he had not intended. Shylocke billed him for the magistrate's time as well, and collected.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
